--TEST--
Core\Db::connect refuses a zero `timeout` before it asks `db.connect` anything, and `{shared: false}` opens nothing the grant does not already cover — `rule:core-classes/db-connection-is-named`, `rule:http-server/no-spelling-for-an-unbounded-wait`, `rule:security/capability-check-at-the-door`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Nothing is configured in this run, so `db.connect` grants nothing and every
// name is ungranted. That is what makes the first line interesting: the option
// is judged anyway, so the sentence a caller gets is about the argument they
// wrote rather than about a capability they would have had to fix first.
try {
    var $db = Core\Db::connect("main", {timeout: 0s});
    echo "opened", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// `rule:http-server/no-spelling-for-an-unbounded-wait` has no spelling for an unbounded wait, and `0s` is that
// spelling said quietly -- so it is refused rather than read as "no deadline",
// which is the reading that would hang a request on a server that never
// answers.
try {
    var $bounded = Core\Db::connect("main", {timeout: 250ms, shared: false});
    echo "opened", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// § 2's `{shared: false}` bypasses memoization inside one request. It is not a
// second door: what it changes is whether an earlier connection may be handed
// back, and the grant answers the same question either way.
try {
    var $dedicated = Core\Db::connect("main", {shared: false});
    echo "opened", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Db::connect: `timeout` must be a positive duration, and this one is 0ns
Core\Db::connect needs the capability `db.connect` for main, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.db]`
Core\Db::connect needs the capability `db.connect` for main, which is not granted
help: grant it in nvs.toml under `[capabilities.db]`
